We consider the following data:
• a surjective map π : V → Θ of R-vector spaces, with fibers Vθ = π −1 (θ);
• a homogeneous polynomial F : V → R of degree n + 1, n ≥ 1, with associated symmetric multilinear map V n+1 → R (x0, . . . , xn) 7→ x0 · . . . · xn, i.e. F(x) = xn+1;
• a convex cone P ⊂ V such that x² · x2 · . . . · xn ≥ 0 for all x ∈ V0 and xi ∈ P. (A.1)
In what follows, we fix θ ∈ Θ and set Pθ := Vθ ∩ P.
Lemma A.2. We have δ(x, y) ≥ 0 for x, y ∈ Pθ. Moreover, F is convex on Pθ, and for every y ∈ Pθ we have that x 7→ δ(x, y) is convex.

Theorem A.3. For all x, y, z ∈ Pθ and t ∈ [0, 1], the following holds:
• quasi-symmetry: δ(x, y) ≈ δ(y, x);
• quasi-triangle inequality: δ(x, z) ≲ δ(x, y) + δ(y, z);
• quadratic estimate: δ(x, (1 − t)x + ty) ≲ t²δ(x, y);
• uniform convexity: [(1 − t)F(x) + tF(y)] − F((1 − t)x + ty) ≳ t(1 − t)δ(x, y).
For any base point x∗ ∈ Pθ and xi, yi, zj ∈ Pθ, the following Hölder estimates further hold:
|(x0 − y0) · (x1 − y1) · z2 · . . . · zn| ≲ δ(x0, y0)^α δ(x1, y1)^α M^(1−2α); (A.5)
|⟨F′(x0) − F′(y0), x1 − y1⟩| ≲ δ(x0, y0)^(1/2) δ(x1, y1)^α M^(1/2−α); (A.6)
with α := 2−n ∈ (0, 1/2] and M = maxξ δ(ξ, x∗), where in each case ξ ranges over the elements of Pθ appearing in the left-hand side of the inequality.

Theorem A. Assume that ω has the orthogonality property. Then:
(i) the image of the Monge–Ampère operator MAω : Dω → M is a dense subspace of M1ω;
(ii) there exists a unique (continuous) quasi-metric δω on M1ω that defines the strong topology of M1ω and such that δω(MAω(φ), MAω(ψ)) = Jω(φ, ψ) for all φ, ψ ∈ Dω;
(iii) the quasi-metric space (M1ω, δω) is complete.
